# Cost controls

Agent teams cost money (a from-scratch build can run tens of dollars). Keep it
predictable — especially when a non-coder is driving.

## Model tiering (enforced via agent frontmatter)

| Tier | Agents | Why |
|---|---|---|
| opus | developer | Hard reasoning — implementation. The expensive one; use sparingly. |
| sonnet | orchestrator, planner, reviewer, test-validator, documentator | Coordination + review. |
| haiku | merger | Mechanical (git merge only). |

Never promote an agent to a bigger model than its role needs.

## Budget cap

Per-request budget: **${{ factory.budget_usd }}** (0 / blank = no hard cap).
When a request is trending past the cap, **pause and ask** before continuing —
in plain language ("This is turning into a bigger job than expected — keep
going?"). Don't silently burn through.

## Keep cold cost down

- Lean `CLAUDE.md` (it's in context every turn); details live in `rules/` loaded
  on demand.
- Keep `settings.json` `enabledPlugins` minimal — a cold cache is expensive even
  for a trivial turn.
- Keep primary context < ~100k tokens; push heavy reads (large files, broad
  greps) into subagents that return only the relevant slice.

## Cheap-first routing

- Try the SIMPLE path before COMPLEX (one developer, no planner/team) when the
  change is small. False-positives toward COMPLEX cost real money.
- Reuse a starter instead of building from scratch (far fewer tokens).

## Surface cost (Layer 1)

When a builder UI is present, show a rough cost after each request so the user
sees what they're spending. Parse it from the runner's result output
(`--output-format stream-json` exposes `total_cost_usd`; note it is cumulative
within a spawn — don't sum per-event).
